--TEST--
`Core\Script::onExit` runs its hooks FIFO as the last user code of a normal end, and the report says `Normal` with status 0 and no error — ADR 0127 §§ 1-2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Registration runs nothing: the two hooks below are registered first and the
// script's own output still comes out first, because the queue is drained
// after the last top-level statement rather than at the call that filled it.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    string $reason = match ($report->reason()) {
        Core\Script\ExitReason::Normal => "Normal",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::ExitCall => "ExitCall",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::UncaughtThrow => "UncaughtThrow",
    };
    // § 2's first row, read off all three fields at once: the last statement
    // ran, so the reason is `Normal`, the status is the 0 the process will
    // carry, and there is no error because only one of the three endings has
    // one.
    echo "first: ", $reason, " ", $report->status(), " ",
        $report->error() == null ? "no error" : "an error", "\n";
});

// Registering twice registers twice — a queue and not a slot, which is what
// lets two unrelated parts of one program each register their own.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    echo "second: ", $report->status(), "\n";
});

// A hook may declare no parameter at all, exactly as a `Core\Fatal` handler
// may: what a hook is handed is decided at the call rather than by its row.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (): void => {
    echo "third: no parameter\n";
});

echo "the script's own last line\n";
--EXPECT--
the script's own last line
first: Normal 0 no error
second: 0
third: no parameter
